From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:57:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 346/488] V2 runner: the sampling family as TileLang kernels

TileLang versions of the V2 runner's Gumbel sampler and temperature, the
sink detector's candidates and draft-zero policy, DSpark's greedy argmax
reductions and greedy verification, each bit-identical to the Triton
kernel it replaces. Nothing launches them yet.

The random stream is Triton 3.5's: Philox-4x32 with ten rounds for
tl.randint, uint_to_uniform_float for tl.rand, and the FP32 Gumbel noise
through libdevice's log1pf and logf as Triton inlines them (its bundled
libdevice, flush-to-zero FMAs), carried as inline PTX in a shared header.
CUDA's own libdevice is another implementation. A block's argmax follows
Triton's reduction order at four warps, which decides the index when NaN
reaches it. The greedy reductions use DSpark's total-order combine, so
their tree is free; 16-byte loads where the rows allow them, as Triton.

The draw is FP32 only (use_fp64_gumbel is not implemented).

The tests compare every kernel with the Triton kernels launched directly,
on logits with ties, infinities and NaN, and the noise on all 2**32
Philox words; the benchmark times each pair per call under CUDA graphs at
DeepSeek V4.1's serving shapes.
